WEBVTT

00:00:01.080 --> 00:00:02.472
You've heard the speech.

00:00:02.562 --> 00:00:03.822
Times are tough.

00:00:03.842 --> 00:00:05.142
Tighten your belts.

00:00:05.162 --> 00:00:07.542
Cut spending, push up interest rates,

00:00:07.562 --> 00:00:09.142
hold wages down.

00:00:09.162 --> 00:00:10.862
Sounds like common sense...

00:00:10.882 --> 00:00:13.102
But austerity has a birthday.

00:00:13.122 --> 00:00:14.862
Economist Clara E. Mattei

00:00:14.882 --> 00:00:15.982
dug through the archives

00:00:16.002 --> 00:00:16.942
and found it,

00:00:16.962 --> 00:00:18.942
right after the First World War.

00:00:18.962 --> 00:00:20.062
And she argues it was

00:00:20.082 --> 00:00:21.862
never really about budgets.

00:00:21.882 --> 00:00:23.752
It was about stopping something.

00:00:25.302 --> 00:00:27.752
Picture Britain in 1915.

00:00:27.882 --> 00:00:29.782
The war is burning through shells

00:00:29.802 --> 00:00:31.862
faster than factories can make them.

00:00:31.882 --> 00:00:32.502
And the government

00:00:32.522 --> 00:00:34.022
doesn't run out of money...

00:00:34.042 --> 00:00:35.792
It runs out of shells.

00:00:35.842 --> 00:00:37.462
So it takes charge:

00:00:37.482 --> 00:00:39.782
what factories make, what things cost,

00:00:39.802 --> 00:00:41.392
what workers are paid.

00:00:41.442 --> 00:00:42.742
Italy does the same.

00:00:42.762 --> 00:00:44.222
And people notice.

00:00:44.242 --> 00:00:46.822
If the economy can be organised for war...

00:00:46.842 --> 00:00:48.422
why not for what people need,

00:00:48.442 --> 00:00:49.952
instead of just for profit?

00:00:51.202 --> 00:00:52.542
When the war ends,

00:00:52.562 --> 00:00:54.512
workers don't want to go back.

00:00:54.642 --> 00:00:57.102
In Britain, union membership doubles,

00:00:57.122 --> 00:00:58.432
to eight million.

00:00:58.562 --> 00:00:59.502
In Glasgow,

00:00:59.522 --> 00:01:01.992
sixty thousand people pack a square,

00:01:02.042 --> 00:01:03.982
demanding a shorter working week.

00:01:04.002 --> 00:01:05.782
The government sends in troops...

00:01:05.802 --> 00:01:06.982
And tanks.

00:01:07.002 --> 00:01:08.302
Coal miners demand public

00:01:08.322 --> 00:01:09.782
ownership of the mines.

00:01:09.802 --> 00:01:11.542
A royal commission backs them.

00:01:11.562 --> 00:01:13.112
The government ignores it.

00:01:14.062 --> 00:01:16.292
In Italy, it goes further.

00:01:16.422 --> 00:01:17.842
In 1919,

00:01:17.862 --> 00:01:20.669
more than a million workers go on strike.

00:01:20.689 --> 00:01:22.722
In 1920, half a million

00:01:22.742 --> 00:01:24.332
take over their factories.

00:01:25.682 --> 00:01:27.902
Now look at it from the owners' side.

00:01:28.032 --> 00:01:29.822
This isn't just about pay.

00:01:29.952 --> 00:01:31.212
It threatens what Mattei

00:01:31.232 --> 00:01:33.212
calls the capital order:

00:01:33.232 --> 00:01:34.652
a few people own the land,

00:01:34.672 --> 00:01:36.492
the factories and the mines...

00:01:36.512 --> 00:01:37.612
and everyone else must

00:01:37.632 --> 00:01:39.852
work for a wage to survive.

00:01:39.872 --> 00:01:42.012
If workers can run things themselves,

00:01:42.032 --> 00:01:43.462
that order could fall.

00:01:44.812 --> 00:01:46.912
So the experts get together.

00:01:47.042 --> 00:01:49.102
Brussels, 1920:

00:01:49.122 --> 00:01:51.182
thirty-nine countries send delegates,

00:01:51.202 --> 00:01:53.582
mostly bankers and treasury officials.

00:01:53.602 --> 00:01:55.262
Guess who isn't in the room?

00:01:55.282 --> 00:01:57.342
Not one trade unionist.

00:01:57.362 --> 00:01:59.542
Genoa, 1922:

00:01:59.562 --> 00:02:00.662
same advice.

00:02:00.682 --> 00:02:01.912
And it's simple.

00:02:01.962 --> 00:02:03.022
Balance the budget.

00:02:03.042 --> 00:02:04.382
Cut public spending.

00:02:04.402 --> 00:02:06.262
Stop creating new money.

00:02:06.282 --> 00:02:08.302
Hand interest rates to central banks,

00:02:08.322 --> 00:02:09.862
out of reach of voters.

00:02:09.882 --> 00:02:11.432
And get back on gold.

00:02:12.582 --> 00:02:15.254
Mattei says the plan has three parts.

00:02:15.344 --> 00:02:16.294
Spending:

00:02:16.384 --> 00:02:17.724
cut public services,

00:02:17.744 --> 00:02:19.484
and tax everyday purchases

00:02:19.504 --> 00:02:21.004
instead of wealth.

00:02:21.024 --> 00:02:21.644
Money:

00:02:21.664 --> 00:02:23.324
make borrowing expensive,

00:02:23.344 --> 00:02:24.454
so businesses slow

00:02:24.504 --> 00:02:26.524
down and jobs disappear.

00:02:26.544 --> 00:02:27.244
Work:

00:02:27.264 --> 00:02:29.964
cut wages, sell off what the public owns,

00:02:29.984 --> 00:02:31.414
weaken the unions.

00:02:32.764 --> 00:02:35.106
The official story sounds reasonable.

00:02:35.196 --> 00:02:36.576
Prices are rising,

00:02:36.596 --> 00:02:39.016
war debts are huge, and a government,

00:02:39.036 --> 00:02:40.096
like a household,

00:02:40.116 --> 00:02:41.816
has to balance its books.

00:02:41.836 --> 00:02:42.616
Right?

00:02:42.636 --> 00:02:43.816
But economists who study

00:02:43.836 --> 00:02:45.576
how modern money works say

00:02:45.596 --> 00:02:46.856
a country that issues its

00:02:46.876 --> 00:02:49.096
own money isn't a household.

00:02:49.116 --> 00:02:51.136
It can't run out of its own money.

00:02:51.156 --> 00:02:52.626
It can run out of workers,

00:02:52.796 --> 00:02:54.136
materials and machines,

00:02:54.156 --> 00:02:55.776
as the war just showed.

00:02:55.796 --> 00:02:56.856
And gold?

00:02:56.876 --> 00:02:58.936
Gold was a limit they chose.

00:02:58.956 --> 00:02:59.976
Going back to it meant

00:02:59.996 --> 00:03:02.386
forcing wages and prices down.

00:03:03.636 --> 00:03:05.006
So why do it?

00:03:05.136 --> 00:03:08.076
Ask Mattei, and her answer is blunt:

00:03:08.096 --> 00:03:09.156
power.

00:03:09.176 --> 00:03:10.676
Take away the alternatives,

00:03:10.696 --> 00:03:11.916
like public housing and

00:03:11.936 --> 00:03:13.356
help for the unemployed,

00:03:13.376 --> 00:03:14.956
and people must take whatever

00:03:14.976 --> 00:03:17.516
job is going, at whatever wage.

00:03:17.536 --> 00:03:19.196
The message to workers:

00:03:19.216 --> 00:03:21.446
work more, consume less.

00:03:22.896 --> 00:03:25.036
That's the argument Mattei makes.

00:03:25.126 --> 00:03:26.306
Austerity was never

00:03:26.326 --> 00:03:27.866
really about the books.

00:03:27.886 --> 00:03:29.506
It was about power...

00:03:29.526 --> 00:03:30.706
Sound familiar?

00:03:30.726 --> 00:03:31.466
But wait.

00:03:31.486 --> 00:03:32.586
If a country can't run

00:03:32.606 --> 00:03:34.066
out of its own money...

00:03:34.086 --> 00:03:36.466
won't creating it just cause inflation?

00:03:36.486 --> 00:03:37.516
That's next.

